// THE RECEIVER RULE TREATS BOTH DECLARATION FORMS ALIKE: a `::` free
// function on the type is refused on an immutable binding exactly as its
// `::` method twin is. The marker on parameter one means the same thing
// whether the function was declared in the struct body or beside it --
// "one function form, one binding rule, nothing added."
//
// This is the corpus half of a must-fail rule: what can be pinned here is
// that the two forms are checked by the SAME gate and not by two gates
// drifting apart. The accepted twin of each rejection lives in the same
// file -- both compile, both land their write -- so if either form's
// mutability check ever widens to accept `:` receivers, or narrows to
// refuse `::` ones, one of the four lines below moves.
//
// Breaks if: the free-function form loses receiver checking (its reject
// becomes an accept and `a.v` changes) or gains an extra one the method
// does not have (`c`/`d` stop compiling).

W = {
    v*:: i32,

    add = (self :: @Self, k: i32) () { self.v = self.v + k; }
}

bump = (w :: W, k: i32) () { w.v = w.v + k * 2; }

main = (env: Env) Res<i32, AllocError> {
    // the accepted twins: `::` bindings reach both forms, each write lands
    a ::= W(v: 1);
    a.bump(10);
    b ::= W(v: 1);
    b.add(10);
    println("free {} method {}", a.v, b.v);
    Ok(0);
}
